    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — IMPORTANT:\nIntersection means the same node reference, not equal values.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\ninterface ListNode {\n  value: number;\n  next: ListNode | null;\n}\n\nfunction getIntersectionNode(\n  first: ListNode | null,\n  second: ListNode | null,\n): ListNode | null {\n  let a = first;\n  let b = second;\n\n  while (a !== b) {\n    a = a === null ? second : a.next;\n    b = b === null ? first : b.next;\n  }\n\n  return a;\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — WITH BUILT-IN Set alternative:\n```ts\ninterface ListNode {\n  value: number;\n  next: ListNode | null;\n}\n\nfunction getIntersectionUsingSet(\n  first: ListNode | null,\n  second: ListNode | null,\n): ListNode | null {\n  const seen = new Set<ListNode>();\n  let current = first;\n\n  while (current !== null) {\n    seen.add(current);\n    current = current.next;\n  }\n\n  current = second;\n\n  while (current !== null) {\n    if (seen.has(current)) return current;\n    current = current.next;\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n```\n\nStep 3 — Example:\nA: 1 → 2 → 8 → 9\nB: 3 → 8 → 9\nThe first shared node object is 8.\n\nStep 4 — Complexity:\nPointer switching: O(n+m) time, O(1) auxiliary space.\nSet method: O(n+m) time, O(n) extra space.",
